$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8b54b919b943d53086b55ebb"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does scalar multiplication by $0$ do to a matrix?","options":["It removes the first column only.","It leaves the matrix unchanged.","It gives the identity matrix.","It gives the zero matrix of the same size."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a5b691861864e7c459fc1c6c"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does scalar multiplication by $1$ do to a matrix?","options":["It transposes the matrix.","It leaves the matrix unchanged.","It changes every entry to $1$.","It gives the zero matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c0f24f0e5bb385e6a1875cca"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is $-A$ in terms of matrix addition?","options":["The additive inverse of $A$.","The transpose of $A$.","The matrix obtained by deleting negative entries.","The identity matrix with the same size as $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"67adb04175f583741e7b88f9"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Compute $2\\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$.","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}2&6\\\\0&-2\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&6\\\\0&-2\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}3&5\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7a0a2bcf59f2e88cf09ca9a8"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&-4\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $\\frac12 A$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac12&\\frac12\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}4&-8\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&-2\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9a8aecd30e1c20d5034925f7"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A$ is a $3\\times4$ matrix, what is the size of $5A$?","options":["$5\\times3$","$4\\times3$","$3\\times4$","$15\\times20$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"299b695f43ce3b62209bdbad"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is $0\\begin{pmatrix}7&-1\\\\2&5\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\2&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}7&-1\\\\2&5\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"18fd2ef62865478be76f938a"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which matrix is $-\\begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\\\3&0\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\-3&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-1&2\\\\-3&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\\\3&-1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-1&-2\\\\3&0\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a6f5d235e4a7b62929cae97c"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How does scalar multiplication interact with matrix addition?","options":["It prevents matrix addition from being defined.","It distributes only over diagonal entries.","It changes addition into transposition.","It distributes over matrix addition."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c5c1a6d954df3fe29cc56ad1"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is matrix scalar multiplication different from matrix multiplication?","options":["Matrix multiplication always changes every entry independently.","One uses a field element to scale entries; the other combines two matrices by rows and columns.","Matrix scalar multiplication is defined only for square matrices, regardless of the specific input chosen.","They are the same operation with different notation."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d1c462f81c80fc14bb8e5ecd"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $\\alpha A$ is the zero matrix, must $A$ be the zero matrix?","options":["Yes, but only when $\\alpha=0$.","No, even if $\\alpha\\ne0$.","Only if $\\alpha\\ne0$.","Yes, regardless of $\\alpha$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5fa16dccc0293d54e6482e13"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$. What is $2(A+B)$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}2&4\\\\6&8\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\2&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&6\\\\8&8\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\\\4&4\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4647521540f2b19db1b3665d"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $3A=\\begin{pmatrix}6&-9\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $A$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}2&-3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}6&-3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}18&-27\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}3&-6\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e749bb62c0b2c4c75c749bad"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression equals $\\alpha A+\\alpha B$ for same-size matrices $A$ and $B$?","options":["$\\alpha(A+B)$","$(\\alpha A)(\\alpha B)$","$A+\\alpha B$ always","$\\alpha AB$ always"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"91800a3ace4b8e2516bc429c"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does scalar multiplication preserve the zero pattern when the scalar is nonzero?","options":["Only diagonal entries are affected.","Nonzero scalars turn every matrix into the identity matrix.","Every nonzero scalar changes all entries to $1$.","Nonzero entries stay nonzero and zero entries stay zero over a field."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d4ec569234eea0b0fe10247e"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Can scalar multiplication change a non-square matrix into a square matrix?","options":["Yes, if the scalar is zero.","Yes, if the scalar is positive.","Only when the matrix has no negative entries, no matter which values are selected.","No, it changes entries but not the number of rows or columns."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1b2aa081e3734c9b9012688c"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For what scalar $\\alpha$ does $\\alpha\\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}6&-3\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["$\\frac13$","$2$","$-3$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3011b6bce9a0214ed5d0f050"},{"id":"la_batch4_matrix_scalar_multiplication_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\2&0\\end{pmatrix}$. Which matrix is $2A-3A$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}5&-5\\\\10&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\\\-2&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\2&0\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e031975615013db32bba8719"}]}}